I headed back to the farm. In real time, it's now 11/24. This games has 
been going on for two weeks! Moreover, there's less than a week left in 
the tournament. I've got to set my priorities. Since this was my first 
tournament, I didn't enter with any particular expectations. As I 
watched my team_ant teammates achieve some nice goals, I began to aspire 
to accomplish something as well. I had some hopes for the Double Top 
Award, but with the tournament 80% over, that didn't seem very likely. I 
had started a ranger on another server, but he hadn't got very far yet. 
And they were both male characters, so I couldn't qualify for the Birdie 
Award, even if I did ascend him. Unless one of them underwent a sex 
change. I had accumulated a lot of amulets from farming, and, sure 
enough, one was an amulet of change. So I decided to put one on and "I 
felt very feminine." The other possibility was to expend all my effort 
on this character and go for the Rogue High Score. I had been watching 
the scoreboard, and so far, no rogue had racked up a very high score. My 
score now was nearly 11,000,000 so I decided to tack on a couple of 
million more and then devoter some time to the ranger. So I slaughter a 
few thousand more puddings and harvested another magic marker and two 
more magic lamps, which only yield 1 silver dagger each. Then I switched 
to playing the ranger for two days, while I watched the rogue scores on 
the scoreboard.

On 11/28 I resumed play with this character. Nothing much had happened 
on the rogue scoreboard and I had managed to complete the quest with the 
ranger character. I racked up 2 million more points and decided to close 
down the farm. I switched to my silver saber and slaughtered the herd 
and the rest of the stay monsters that had been lurking around the 
periphery. So now it's time to pack for the planes. This always takes me 
a long time since you accumulate a lot of stuff while farming and 
deciding what to leave behind is very hard, for me a least. Normally, I 
polymorph all the useless stuff until it becomes useful or disappears. 
Since I had managed to get thru the rogue quest without polyselfing, I 
had decided to try to go polyless as well. I mean, I'd already broken 
every other conduct, so it seemed like the least I could do. So I wound 
up leaving dozens, if not hunderes, of scrolls, potions, wands, rings, 
and other goodies behind in the chest. I used up all the potions I could 
alchemize and got my HP up to over 1350 and got a batch of about 50 
blessed diluted smokey potions but only got 1 wish and 1 more silver 
dagger. I refreshed all my spells and set off to see the wizard at turn 
119533.

Rodney summoned up a small group of nasties, but I had genocided 
everything I could that worried me, so I took care of them and him 
without too much trouble. I had also used Stormbringer on myself a few 
times to keep my level at 18 or below, and hopefully avoid some of the 
nastier monsters. I picked up the Book of the Dead at turn 119772 and 
level 'ported to level 51. I went to the VS and performed the invocation 
at turn 119783 and entered Moloch's Sanctum. I waded through the 
graveyard without too much trouble. The silver saber works a treat on 
those clowns. When I tried to enter the main area on the left hand side, 
I got surrounded. I wasn't taking much damage (AC -87 will do that for 
you), but I always get very uptight when I have to hit the spacebar 15 
or 20 times before I get a move in. But I backed into the hallway again 
and picked them off one at a time. By now I'm two weaponing Magicbane 
and the silver saber and taking out most opponents pretty easily. I used 
a wand of secret door detection to find the temple entrance and went in. 
The priest again summoned some nasties, but again, he and they went down 
pretty quickly. Moloch's lightning bolts were, as usual, pretty much a 
joke and I picked up the Amulet of Yendor at turn 119888. I verified its 
authenticity by trying to put it in my bag and named it "The Real 
McCoy". Quaffed a cursed potion of gain level and I'm off to the planes.

The mysterious force wasn't too bad: 46 -> 47, 46 -> 46, 46 -> 47, 43 -> 
44, 42 -> 42, 42 -> 42, 42 -> 43, and 38 -> 38. Then, smooth sailing. I 
had quite a few cursed potions of gain level from my alchemy, so I used 
them liberally on the no teleport levels, such as Balzebuub's, 
Asmodeus', Juiblex's, VotD, Castle, and Medusa. I got back to base camp 
on 9 (didn't note the time), transferred all the stuff I wanted to take 
to the planes to my bag of holding, grabbed my first aid kit and set off.

The plane of earth was a breeze as usual. Dropped Rodney with a wand of 
death, used a ? of gold detection while confused to find the portal, and 
dug my way there. I dropped all my wands of digging and my trusty 
pick-axe, swapped my speed boots for levitation boots, my robe for a 
cloak of displacement, and stepped through.

The plane of air was, as usual, a bitch. I wish someone would explain 
the secret to me. The plane of air is the main reason I farm puddings. 
Those damn air elementals just grind me up. Only by having upwards of a 
1000 HP and/or a half physical damage artifact such as the MKOT, can I 
hope to survive. Take a step, get engulfed, hack my way out, and repeat. 
I know I must be doing something wrong, but I haven't a clue what it is. 
Anyway, I used another ? of GD to find the portal, and oh so slowly 
worked my way there. Finally make it and step through.

The plane of fire is usually much easier than the plane of air.
Use the ? of GD to find the portal (color is an immense help hear, how 
do you do it without color?) and dodge and weave your way there. And 
step through to water.

With the ";"'s genocided, plane of water is a snap. Finding the portal 
is a little annoying because it keeps moving around, but it generally 
goes pretty quickly. The snakes and water elementals don't seem to be 
very much of a problem. The portal eventually hoves into view, and it's 
on to the Astral Plane.

I arrived on the Astral Plane at turn 121830, 1942 turns after picking 
up the Amulet. I put on my blindfold and checked out the opposition: 
Pestilence to the right, Death in the center, and Famine to the left. I 
landed to the right, so decided to go right first. I had 2 wands of 
death, so I figured it was easier to face Pesty and Famine before Death. 
I zapped Pesty with the wand of death and used a couple of zaps of a 
teleport wand to clear my way to the right hand altar and it was 
neutral. Damn! worst possible outcome. I still had my wand of wishing 
from the Castle with all 5 wishes left and had decided I would use it to 
wish for a helm of opposite alignment, if the situation should present 
itself. This was more to do with the tournament awards than with any 
ethics of nethack play. My first character to survive a reasonable time 
was this human rogue, who had to be chaotic. My second was an elf 
ranger, who also had to be chaotic. So to qualify for the Double Top 
award (which was now out of reach due to lack of time, but that was not 
the situation when I made the original decision) one of them would have 
to convert. I've never used a helm of opposite alignment, but my 
understanding is that you can't wind up neutral from using one, so a 
neutral altar was useless. So it's off to the left hand altar. I had to 
zap Pesty again on the way out.

I made it to the left hand altar via much the same actions: zap Famine 
with the wand of death and clear a path with a wand of teleport. I also 
cast charm monster once when surrounded by a bunch of lower level 
monsters to serve as meat shields. The left hand altar turned out to be 
chaotic so I didn't have to face the difficult moral dilemma of using a 
helm of opposite alignment or not. Normally it would never be a question 
for me. I'm a pudding farmer, so I have almost no ethics, but I would 
never consider using a helm of opposite alignment outside the 
tournament, even if it meant dying with the amulet on the Astral Plane. 
I don't know why I find it so distasteful, but I do. Anyhow, I whistled 
in whatever pets I might have acquired along the way, and, in a moment 
of supreme irony, a black pudding showed up. However, it was still 
eating (who knew black puddings ate) as I offered the amulet, so 
apparently didn't ascend with me to the demigod bar.

